{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"pwd\nls -ld .venv 2>/dev/null || true\ncat pyproject.toml\nsed -n '1,260p' STATE.md\nsed -n '1,320p' literature_review.md\nsed -n '1,320p' resources.md\nsed -n '1,300p' planning.md\nsed -n '1,320p' papers/deep_read_notes.md\nsed -n '1,220p' datasets/README.md\nsed -n '1,240p' code/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"/workspaces/llms-are-bad-at-keeping-obvious-secrets-72ad-codex-gpt-5-6-sol\ndrwxrwsrwx 4 neurico 27500 8 Oct  3 01:56 .venv\n[project]\nname = \"research-workspace\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\ndescription = \"Research workspace for experiments\"\nrequires-python = \">=3.10\"\ndependencies = [\n    \"arxiv>=4.0.1\",\n    \"httpx>=0.28.1\",\n    \"pypdf>=6.19.0\",\n    \"requests>=2.34.2\",\n]\n\n[build-system]\nrequires = [\"hatchling\"]\nbuild-backend = \"hatchling.build\"\n\n[tool.uv]\npackage = false\n# Research State\n\n- Current phase: `experiment_runner`\n- Pipeline completed: `False`\n\n## Previous phases\n\nresource_finder (succeeded)\n\n## Current phase context\n\n- Phase: `experiment_runner`\n- Status: `in_progress`\n- Started: `2026-10-03T02:15:31.997987Z`\n- Next steps:\n  - Validate the report and experimental artifacts before finalizing.\n\n## Workspace check\n\n- Root: `/workspaces/llms-are-bad-at-keeping-obvious-secrets-72ad-codex-gpt-5-6-sol`\n- Directory usable: `True`\n\n## Output validation\n\nNo phase output validation recorded yet.\n\n## Agent notes\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START -->\n### resource_finder\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:resource_finder -->\nPhase complete (2026-10-03). Downloaded, validated, chunked, and fully read 12 papers (all 10 specified plus semantic-leakage and inadvertent-context-leakage follow-ups). Gathered three dataset collections and five official repositories; all data load, repo objects verify, and Python sources compile. Evidence: `papers/README.md`, `papers/deep_read_notes.md`, `datasets/README.md`, `code/README.md`, `literature_review.md`, and `resources.md`.\n\nDirection budget is frozen in `planning.md`: (1) causal behavioral outline factorial, (2) tokenwise secret-representation mediation, and (3) causal representation removal/steering. Rejected scaling, fine-tuning, private-memory, standalone rebound, and large human-first directions are documented there.\n\nNext phase: implement a crossed story corpus with held-out-secret splits; reproduce no-outline 2AFC leakage; add secret-blind/secret-aware/self-generated/decoy outlines; then adapt `code/response-planning/` for residual collection and controlled erasure. Preserve narrative utility metrics and random-direction controls.\n\nUncertainty: the primary Holtzman–West arXiv v1 exposes no official code; its design must be reconstructed from the paper. Paper-finder returned HTTP 500 twice, so discovery used arXiv/API, references, and official repositories. GPU/model-dependent repository examples were inspected and compiled but not executed.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:resource_finder -->\n\n### experiment_runner\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_START:experiment_runner -->\nUpdate this section at the end of the `experiment_runner` phase.\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END:experiment_runner -->\n\n<!-- NEURICO_AGENT_NOTES_END -->\n# Literature Review\n\n## Review Scope\n\n**Research question.** When an LLM writes while holding a secret it must not reveal, does an explicit outline reduce nonliteral leakage, and is any reduction mediated by—then causally dependent on—the secret's internal representation during prose generation?\n\n**Inclusion criteria.** English-language work from 2023–2026 was included if it (a) measures unintended semantic/contextual influence on generation, (b) probes or intervenes on latent secret/future-response information, (c) separates narrative planning from realization, or (d) provides a directly reusable privacy/rebound benchmark. All ten user-specified papers were mandatory.\n\n**Exclusion criteria.** Training-data memorization, ordinary jailbreak/prompt extraction, generic privacy surveys, and planning papers without measurable generation-state or story relevance were excluded. Closely duplicative follow-ups were screened out when they added neither a new mechanism nor reusable resource.\n\n**Sources.** Paper-finder was attempted first, followed by arXiv/API search, author and GitHub pages, reference chasing, and targeted web search across Semantic Scholar/Papers with Code/Google Scholar discovery surfaces. Official paper and repository pages were used for final metadata.\n\n## Search Log\n\n| Date | Query/source | Result | Decision |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026-10-03 | Paper-finder diligent: “large language models secret leakage fiction foreshadowing outline planning generation” | HTTP 500 twice; 0 returned | Recorded failure; manual fallback |\n| 2026-10-03 | Ten supplied arXiv IDs via arXiv API | 10 records | All included and full-text screened |\n| 2026-10-03 | “semantic leakage language models” / arXiv and web | Gonen et al. plus a small-model follow-up | Included foundational Gonen et al.; excluded redundant scaling follow-up |\n| 2026-10-03 | “inadvertent context leakage language models” / recent arXiv | Fairoze et al. (August 2026) | Included; direct post-specification evidence |\n| 2026-10-03 | Paper references and “response planning activations”, “ironic negation”, “story iterative planning” | Probe, rebound, and narrative-planning lines | Relevant papers already covered by supplied set; official code located |\n\n## Screening Results\n\n| Group | Title | Abstract | Full text | Outcome |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 10 supplied papers | Include | Include | Include | Mandatory and directly/adjacently relevant |\n| Gonen et al., semantic leakage | Include | Include | Include | Added as foundational behavioral baseline |\n| Fairoze et al., inadvertent context leakage | Include | Include | Include | Added as closest new mechanism/threat-model result |\n| Smilga, *Scaling Down Semantic Leakage* | Maybe | Include | Not required | Excluded: primarily repeats size comparison; low marginal information for outline mechanism |\n| Prompt injection/training-data extraction literature | Maybe | Exclude | — | Different threat model: deliberate extraction or memorization |\n| General story-generation/planning surveys | Maybe | Exclude | — | No secret-leakage or internal-state test |\n\n## Research Area Overview\n\nThe target phenomenon is narrower than ordinary prompt extraction or direct privacy failure. A model can obey the literal instruction not to say a secret while allowing that secret to influence topic, imagery, wording, length, or other output statistics. Three adjacent literatures converge on this problem: behavioral semantic leakage, latent-knowledge/response-planning probes, and narrative planning. Together they support the hypothesis that a secret may remain active throughout generation, but they do not yet test whether an external outline reduces leakage by offloading future preparation, nor whether causally removing the secret representation removes the leak.\n\nHoltzman and West (2026) provide the closest behavioral result. They give models a secret word, ask them not to reveal it, and use another model in an order-balanced binary discrimination task to infer the secret from stories. The secret never appears literally, yet five frontier models leak thematically at rates up to 79.3%. Stronger “actively hide it” instructions reverse the signal below chance because systematic avoidance is itself diagnostic; a decoy redirects some signal. Leakage disappears in short stock jokes, implicating open-ended content choice rather than inevitable token copying. The paper does not manipulate story planning or model internals.\n\nGonen et al. (2025) establish the broader category of semantic leakage: unrelated prompt concepts affect completions more than matched controls. Their 109-example suite yields significant leakage in 13 GPT/Llama variants and in multilingual, cross-lingual, story, and recipe settings. Human agreement is high enough to validate the effect, while embedding metrics only moderately track item-level judgments. This motivates matched controls and human/model 2AFC evaluation, but it does not involve an instruction to keep a secret.\n\nFairoze et al. (2026), published after the primary story-leakage paper, show that benign outputs can encode protected in-context values through model-specific statistical channels. Adaptive black-box decoders recover two-digit secrets perfectly for some models and four-digit secrets at 82% exact match. The authors propose suppression-induced probability redistribution: a model avoids the protected value, producing a value-dependent output distribution. Suppression correlates with leakage across eight models (rho 0.95), and more emphatic confidentiality instructions can worsen recovery. This is strong evidence that “trying not to say X” can preserve or amplify an X-dependent channel, though the main controlled task is numeric and adversarial decoding is trained per model.\n\n## Key Papers\n\n### Behavioral Leakage and Privacy\n\n**Holtzman & West (2026), “Can You Keep a Secret?”** The study crosses 15 secrets with story, essay, and joke tasks under secret-present, no-secret, active-hiding, and decoy controls. Leakage is scored through blinded 2AFC discrimination with both candidate orders, binomial testing, and multiple-comparison correction. Cross-model judge transfer and within-family scaling argue against a single evaluator artifact. Key limitations are a small secret set, reliance on model judges, and no released code link in the inspected v1 PDF. It defines the primary behavioral endpoint for this project.\n\n**Mireshghallah et al. (2023), ConfAIde.** Four tiers move from sensitivity ratings to contextual norms, theory-of-mind sharing, and meeting summaries. Tier 3 contains 270 synthetic scenarios and Tier 4 contains 20 transcripts. GPT-4 leaks in 22% of Tier-3 generations and produces inappropriate flows in 39% of Tier-4 summaries; a chain-of-thought prompt slightly worsens both leakage and utility. Low annotation agreement on some norm judgments (Krippendorff alpha 0.07–0.34) warns that privacy evaluation can be subjective. ConfAIde is best used as a transfer and utility benchmark, since it measures explicit contextual disclosure rather than subtle thematic influence.\n\n**Mann et al. (2025), White Bear.** ReboundBench has 5,000 templated negation prompts plus 500 matched multi-question prompts. It measures forbidden-token log probability after semantic, syntactic, or repetitive distractors. Semantic load generally strengthens rebound, while repetition can support suppression. Attention-head ablations on Llama-3-8B-Instruct suggest a sparse middle-layer contribution. The outcome is next-token salience, not story leakage, and the causal result is narrow; the matched positive/neutral/negative controls remain valuable.\n\n**Fairoze et al. (2026), Inadvertent Context Leakage.** Six decoder strategies combine frequency statistics and Qwen LoRA classifiers, with shuffled-label controls. The work also tests digit-stripped length/style/embedding channels, confidentiality wording, co-present distractors, and semantic predicates over user memories. It provides a strong threat-model and split discipline, but trained adaptive attackers answer a different question from ordinary reader-perceived foreshadowing. Its main lesson is to evaluate suppression and active hiding as potentially informative channels, not merely mitigations.\n\n### Internal State, Secret Elicitation, and Planning\n\n**Cywiński et al. (2025), latent knowledge.** Gemma-2-9B models are QLoRA-trained to play Taboo while hiding one of 20 single-token secrets. An external model reaches 95% Pass@10 guessing, postgame token forcing reaches 70%, final-layer logit lens reaches 75% top-5, and Gemma Scope SAE features reach 55% top-5. These results demonstrate accessible secret information but rely on a small, fine-tuned model organism and manually mapped SAE features.\n\n**Cywiński et al. (2025), Eliciting Secret Knowledge.** The expanded benchmark uses three model-organism families: Taboo, secret-selection/concealment, and user-gender knowledge. Prefill attacks often exceed 95% on the latter two settings; logit-lens and SAE methods consistently beat input/output baselines but are weaker. For Taboo, activation-token elicitation reaches 85.1% versus 42.1% for ordinary input/output elicitation. This establishes methods and controls for locating secret representations, but it asks whether knowledge is recoverable, not whether it causally shapes prose.\n\n**Dong et al. (2025), Emergent Response Planning.** Linear/MLP probes predict response length, reasoning steps, final answers, correctness, consistency, and animal characters from prompt-end residual states across eight 7–8B models. Decodability scales and often follows a high–low–high temporal trajectory. Greedy decoding and prompt correlations limit causal interpretation. For this project the important contribution is the reusable activation-collection/probing pipeline and the explicit warning that decodability alone is correlational.\n\n**Wu et al. (2024), FutureGPT2.** Myopic training removes gradients from future-token losses to earlier computations. Synthetic tasks show necessary “pre-caching,” whereas small-model natural-language results show only a modest myopia gap and near-zero local bonus, consistent with semantic breadcrumbs rather than deliberate plans. The gap grows with Pythia scale up to 2.8B. Thus an outline might reduce deliberate pre-caching without removing ordinary associative breadcrumbs; behavioral and activation measurements must be collected together.\n\n**Luo et al. (2026), Stable Internal Beliefs.** Branch probes during 20-questions games find that once a target belief drifts it nearly always remains drifted, and KL-to-initial-belief fine-tuning reduces drift. The “internal belief” is inferred behaviorally, so instability may partly reflect the probe response. This motivates measuring the secret with both activation probes and behavioral counterfactuals.\n\n**Baldelli et al. (2026), Private Working Memory.** A formal result shows that a persistent private state cannot be guaranteed when all durable state is exposed through a public transcript. Hangman and diagnostic tasks improve with explicit private-memory channels. This is relevant boundary-setting: private state solves a multi-turn memory architecture problem, while the present question concerns offloading prose planning into a public outline.\n\n### Narrative Planning\n\n**Xie & Riedl (2024), Suspenseful Stories.** Their pipeline separates background generation, iterative action–failure plotting, and detail elaboration with planted clues and delayed explanations. Human evaluation favors the full method over direct ChatGPT generation for suspense (84.9%), novelty (76.6%), and enjoyment (67.9%), while ablations show both outline and detail stages matter. This is direct evidence that plan/prose separation changes narrative behavior. However, every stage is model-generated; it does not independently manipulate what information the plan contains. The proposed experiment therefore needs secret-blind, secret-aware, and self-generated outlines.\n\n## Common Methodologies and Baselines\n\nThe literature suggests four complementary measurement families:\n\n- **Matched behavioral controls:** secret-present versus no-secret, ordinary suppression versus active hiding, and real-secret versus decoy. Hold story brief, length target, model, and decoding settings fixed.\n- **Blinded discrimination:** order-balanced 2AFC inference is more sensitive than literal-string checks and avoids requiring an exhaustive paraphrase ontology. Exact/free guesses add ecological validity.\n- **Representation probes:** collect residual streams at prompt end and throughout generation, use simple preregistered classifiers, evaluate on held-out secrets, and include shuffled labels and prompt-only baselines.\n- **Causal interventions:** ablate or project out a learned direction, compare against norm-matched random/unrelated directions, and jointly measure target reduction and utility damage.\n\nStandard baselines should be: no secret, secret with no outline, secret-blind fixed outline, secret-aware fixed outline, self-generated outline, active hiding, and decoy. Random subspace interventions and sign-reversed steering are essential for the white-box study. A model-size sweep is secondary because prior work already finds larger or more capable models leak more.\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n\nThe primary endpoint should be 2AFC secret-identification accuracy with candidate order counterbalanced. Analyze it with mixed-effects logistic regression using outline condition and candidate order as fixed effects and secret/story brief as random effects. Report effect sizes and bootstrap confidence intervals, not only significance.\n\nSecondary leakage metrics are exact secret guess, top-k guess, calibrated judge log odds, and matched semantic similarity. Mechanism metrics are cross-validated secret-probe accuracy/AUROC, layerwise decodability, and trial-level association between decodability and leakage. Intervention success requires both reduced decodability and reduced behavioral leakage. Utility metrics should cover plan adherence, coherence, suspense/foreshadowing quality, diversity, length, and refusal rate. Layer/time scans require false-discovery correction or a preregistered region of interest.\n\n## Datasets in the Literature\n\nThe downloaded Semantic Leakage suite supplies 109 handcrafted semantic examples and categories. ReboundBench supplies 5,000 single-token forbidden-concept prompts plus 500 matched negative/neutral/positive prompts. ConfAIde supplies privacy-sensitivity, contextual-integrity, sharing, and meeting-summary scenarios. None directly implements the required crossed story-outline experiment, so the primary dataset should be generated from screened concrete secrets and reusable story briefs. Existing datasets are best used for concept seeding, controls, and transfer validation.\n\n## Gaps and Opportunities\n\n1. No paper tests whether an externally supplied outline reduces involuntary story leakage while holding secret exposure constant.\n2. Existing planning probes and secret elicitation studies are correlational; story-level causal mediation has not been shown.\n3. Active hiding can invert or amplify signal, so “lower semantic similarity” alone is not evidence that information was removed.\n4. Most classifiers split by example, not by secret identity, allowing concept-specific memorization to inflate apparent generalization.\n5. Privacy and narrative quality are rarely evaluated jointly; an intervention can appear safe by making prose generic, incoherent, or refusal-heavy.\n\n## Recommendations for the Experiment\n\nFollow the three ranked directions in `planning.md`. Begin with a crossed behavioral factorial using an open 7–14B instruction model and one frontier API replication. The decisive contrast is no outline versus an externally supplied secret-blind outline. Then collect tokenwise residual activations on the same trials and test whether reduced decodability mediates any behavioral effect. Finally, remove or steer the secret subspace during prose generation with random-direction controls.\n\nUse 30–50 secrets across semantic categories, at least eight reusable briefs per secret, and held-out-secret splits. Use multiple independent judges and a small blinded human audit. Preserve full prompts, outlines, seeds, model revisions, and decoding parameters. Treat semantic similarity and forbidden-token rebound as supporting metrics only; the central claim requires story-level inference and quality-preserving causal intervention.\n\n## Key Citations\n\n- Holtzman & West, 2026, [arXiv:2605.10794](https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.10794) — primary involuntary story-leakage result.\n- Gonen et al., 2025, [arXiv:2408.06518](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.06518) — semantic-leakage definition and matched evaluation.\n- Fairoze et al., 2026, [arXiv:2608.19857](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.19857) — adaptive decoding and suppression hypothesis.\n- Dong et al., 2025, [arXiv:2502.06258](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.06258) — response-planning activation probes.\n- Cywiński et al., 2025, [arXiv:2510.01070](https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01070) — public secret-elicitation benchmark.\n- Xie & Riedl, 2024, [arXiv:2402.17119](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.17119) — explicit story plan/prose separation.\n# Resources Catalog\n\n## Summary\n\nThis workspace contains 12 validated papers, three locally available dataset collections, and five official research repositories. The resources support a behavioral story-outline experiment, activation mediation analysis, and causal representation intervention.\n\n## Papers\n\nTotal PDFs: **12**. Every PDF was parsed successfully and read in full from three-page chunks. See `papers/README.md` and `papers/deep_read_notes.md`.\n\n| Paper | Year | File | Key information |\n|---|---:|---|---|\n| Holtzman & West, *Can You Keep a Secret?* | 2026 | `papers/2605.10794_keep_a_secret.pdf` | Primary thematic story-leakage benchmark |\n| Fairoze et al., *Inadvertent Context Leakage* | 2026 | `papers/2608.19857_inadvertent_context_leakage.pdf` | Adaptive black-box leakage and suppression mechanism |\n| Gonen et al., *Semantic Leakage* | 2025 | `papers/2408.06518_semantic_leakage.pdf` | Matched control metric and human validation |\n| Cywiński et al., *Towards Eliciting Latent Knowledge* | 2025 | `papers/2505.14352_latent_knowledge_mech_interp.pdf` | Logit-lens and SAE secret elicitation |\n| Cywiński et al., *Eliciting Secret Knowledge* | 2025 | `papers/2510.01070_eliciting_secret_knowledge.pdf` | Expanded public secret-elicitation benchmark |\n| Dong et al., *Emergent Response Planning* | 2025 | `papers/2502.06258_emergent_response_planning.pdf` | Future-property activation probes |\n| Wu et al., *Plan Ahead for Future Tokens?* | 2024 | `papers/2404.00859_plan_ahead.pdf` | Myopic training and pre-caching/breadcrumb distinction |\n| Xie & Riedl, *Creating Suspenseful Stories* | 2024 | `papers/2402.17119_suspenseful_stories.pdf` | Explicit plot/prose separation and narrative evaluation |\n| Mann et al., *White Bear* | 2025 | `papers/2511.12381_white_bear_rebound.pdf` | Rebound benchmark and attention-head ablation |\n| Mireshghallah et al., *ConfAIde* | 2023 | `papers/2310.17884_confaide.pdf` | Direct privacy leakage and utility benchmark |\n| Baldelli et al., *Private Working Memory* | 2026 | `papers/2601.06973_private_working_memory.pdf` | Boundary case: persistent private state |\n| Luo et al., *Stable Internal Beliefs* | 2026 | `papers/2603.25187_stable_internal_beliefs.pdf` | Behavioral probes and belief drift |\n\n## Datasets\n\nTotal downloaded collections: **3**. Data files are present locally but excluded from Git; exact re-download commands and schemas are in `datasets/README.md`.\n\n| Name | Source | Size | Task | Location | Notes |\n|---|---|---:|---|---|---|\n| Semantic Leakage Prompt Suite | [author-hosted](https://gonenhila.github.io/files/semantic_leakage_prompts.txt) | 140 lines / 10.6 KB | Matched semantic completion | `datasets/semantic_leakage/` | 109 paper examples organized as prompt variants |\n| ConfAIde | [GitHub](https://github.com/skywalker023/confaide/tree/main/benchmark) | 510 KB across 8 files | Contextual privacy | `datasets/confaide/` | Tiers 1–4, labels, controls, transcripts |\n| ReboundBench | [GitHub](https://github.com/cesium132dot9/Dont-Think-of-the-White-Bear/tree/main/data) | 5,500 CSV rows / 634 KB | Negation/rebound | `datasets/reboundbench/` | 5,000 primary and 500 matched multi-question records |\n\n## Code Repositories\n\nTotal repositories: **5**. All are shallow clones at recorded commits and pass Python bytecode compilation. See `code/README.md` for requirements and entry points.\n\n| Name | URL | Purpose | Location | Status |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ConfAIde | [GitHub](https://github.com/skywalker023/confaide) | Privacy benchmark/evaluators | `code/confaide/` | Compiles; API/model execution not attempted |\n| Emergent Response Planning | [GitHub](https://github.com/niconi19/Emergent-Response-Planning-in-LLMs) | Residual collection and probes | `code/response-planning/` | Compiles; needs models/GPU |\n| Eliciting Secrets | [GitHub](https://github.com/EmilRyd/eliciting-secrets) | Secret model organisms, logit lens, SAE | `code/eliciting-secrets/` | Compiles; needs heavyweight ML stack/GPU |\n| White Bear | [GitHub](https://github.com/cesium132dot9/Dont-Think-of-the-White-Bear) | Rebound and head ablations | `code/white-bear/` | Compiles; datasets load |\n| FutureGPT2 | [GitHub](https://github.com/wiwu2390/FutureGPT2-public) | Myopic/future-token training | `code/futuregpt2/` | Compiles; no pinned environment |\n\n## Resource Gathering Notes\n\n### Search Strategy\n\nThe required paper-finder service was tried first in diligent mode after installing its missing `httpx` dependency. The service returned HTTP 500 twice, leaving two empty diagnostic JSONL files under `paper_search_results/`. The fallback search used arXiv metadata/API, the ten user-provided arXiv identifiers, forward/related-paper web search, reference lists, author pages, GitHub, and Hugging Face links. Search concepts included *involuntary information leakage*, *semantic leakage*, *secret elicitation*, *response planning activations*, *future-token planning*, *ironic negation/rebound*, *contextual privacy*, and *story outline planning*.\n\nTwo high-relevance papers not in the original list were added: Gonen et al. (semantic leakage) and Fairoze et al. (inadvertent context leakage). The latter appeared after the primary May 2026 paper and directly strengthens the suppression-mechanism evidence.\n\n### Selection Criteria\n\nResources were retained when they directly measured nonliteral secret influence, supplied a mechanism/probe/intervention, separated planning from prose, or provided a reusable benchmark/codebase. Preference went to official arXiv PDFs and author repositories with data. Generic prompt-injection, training-data memorization, and broad privacy surveys were excluded because they do not isolate involuntary thematic leakage.\n\n### Challenges and Workarounds\n\n- Paper-finder was unavailable server-side; manual multi-source search and arXiv API metadata replaced it.\n- The primary Holtzman–West v1 paper says code is redacted for review and the arXiv record exposes no official repository. Its prompt design and analyses were reconstructed from the paper, but no unofficial substitute was cloned.\n- Several repositories require large model weights, credentials, or GPUs. Validation was limited to source/README inspection, data loading, commit pinning, and compilation.\n- ReboundBench's README claims MIT but the cloned snapshot lacks a root license file; its downloaded data should be treated as research-only until clarified.\n- No established dataset crosses secrets, story briefs, and outline provenance. The primary dataset must therefore be generated, with existing resources used as concept seeds and transfer evaluations.\n\n## Recommendations for Experiment Design\n\n1. **Primary data:** Generate a crossed story corpus from 30–50 screened secrets and at least eight reusable briefs, with no-outline, secret-blind outline, secret-aware outline, self-outline, decoy, and no-secret conditions.\n2. **Behavioral baseline:** Reproduce Holtzman–West order-balanced 2AFC leakage before adding outlines. Include exact/free guesses and no-secret controls.\n3. **Mechanism code:** Adapt `code/response-planning/` for tokenwise residual collection, then borrow secret configurations from `code/eliciting-secrets/`.\n4. **Causal tests:** Add projection removal/steering with norm-matched random and unrelated-concept controls. White Bear provides a useful ablation template, but its forbidden-token metric should remain secondary.\n5. **Utility:** Score plan adherence, coherence, suspense, length, diversity, and refusal. ConfAIde can supply a separate privacy/utility transfer check.\n6. **Statistics:** Split by secret identity, use mixed-effects logistic models, preregister layer/time regions or correct multiple comparisons, and report uncertainty/effect sizes.\n\nThe final three-direction ranking and rejected alternatives are recorded in `planning.md`.\n# Research Direction Ranking\n\nScores use a 1–5 scale. The total weights literature support (25%), hypothesis relevance (30%), expected information gain (25%), and implementation feasibility (20%). The search space is frozen to the top three directions below unless new evidence invalidates this ranking.\n\n| Rank | Direction | Literature | Relevance | Information gain | Feasibility | Weighted total | Decision |\n|---:|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| 1 | Causal behavioral outline experiment | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5 | 5.00 | Keep |\n| 2 | Tokenwise secret-representation mediation | 4 | 5 | 5 | 4 | 4.55 | Keep |\n| 3 | Causal representation removal/steering | 4 | 5 | 5 | 3 | 4.35 | Keep |\n| 4 | Broad model-size scaling survey | 4 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 3.10 | Reject: expensive and mostly replicates established scaling results before testing the outline mechanism |\n| 5 | Fine-tune models to keep secrets | 3 | 3 | 3 | 2 | 2.80 | Reject: training introduces capability and data confounds; mitigation is premature before mechanism identification |\n| 6 | Multi-turn private-memory architecture | 4 | 2 | 3 | 2 | 2.75 | Reject: tests persistent hidden state, not whether a public outline offloads prose planning |\n| 7 | Ironic-rebound next-token benchmark | 4 | 3 | 2 | 4 | 3.20 | Reject as a standalone direction: useful supporting diagnostic, but not the story-level outcome in the hypothesis |\n| 8 | Large human creative-writing study first | 3 | 4 | 3 | 2 | 3.10 | Reject for the first pass: costly and slow; use blinded model judges plus a small human validation after effect sizing |\n\n## Direction 1 — Causal Behavioral Outline Experiment\n\nUse a within-secret factorial design with identical story briefs and decoding settings:\n\n1. no outline;\n2. externally supplied secret-blind outline;\n3. externally supplied secret-aware outline;\n4. model-generated outline;\n5. decoy-outline control;\n6. no-secret control.\n\nThe crucial comparison is no outline versus a secret-blind fixed outline. It tests the offloading hypothesis without adding a second exposure to the secret. Secret-aware and self-generated outlines identify whether planning helps because it structures prose or hurts because it entangles the secret with the plan. Primary outcome: order-balanced, blinded 2AFC secret identification on held-out stories, with exact/free secret guesses and semantic similarity as secondary outcomes. Measure coherence, plan adherence, suspense, and style so suppression cannot masquerade as success by destroying story quality.\n\nEvidence: Holtzman and West (2026) establish story-level leakage and appropriate controls; Gonen et al. (2025) validate matched semantic-leakage measurement; Xie and Riedl (2024) show explicit iterative plots improve suspense and consistency; ConfAIde shows privacy prompts can trade off with utility.\n\n## Direction 2 — Tokenwise Secret-Representation Mediation\n\nOn an open-weight instruction model, collect residual-stream activations at prompt end, outline tokens, and fixed prose-generation landmarks. Train cross-validated probes to identify the secret from held-out secret classes, with shuffled-label, prompt-only, lexical-overlap, and random-direction controls. Test the preregistered mediation chain:\n\n`outline condition → secret decodability during prose → behavioral leakage`.\n\nEstimate decodability trajectories per layer and token position, and relate trial-level probe scores to story leakage while controlling for story length, outline adherence, and secret frequency. Treat probe success as accessibility, not causality.\n\nEvidence: Dong et al. (2025) demonstrate future-response decodability and its temporal profile; Cywiński et al. (2025) show hidden secrets are recoverable with logit-lens/SAE methods; Wang et al. (2026) warn that behaviorally probed internal states can drift; Wu et al. (2024) distinguish deliberate pre-caching from ordinary breadcrumbs.\n\n## Direction 3 — Causal Representation Removal/Steering\n\nLearn a secret subspace on disjoint training secrets, then intervene only during prose generation using at least two methods (for example, mean-difference projection removal and LEACE/INLP-style erasure). Compare with norm-matched random subspaces, unrelated-concept removal, sign-reversed steering, and prompt-level no-secret controls. Evaluate whether intervention lowers both probe decodability and 2AFC leakage while preserving plan adherence and prose quality. Apply interventions across layer/time windows to localize when secret information is causally used.\n\nThis direction answers the strongest mechanism claim: whether removing the active concept removes leakage. It should follow the behavioral and correlational studies because aggressive erasure can damage generation nonspecifically.\n\nEvidence: the elicitation papers show recoverable secret representations but remain largely correlational; White Bear provides a narrow head-ablation precedent; inadvertent-context leakage links suppression to detectable output distortions but does not manipulate internal representations.\n\n## Minimal First Experiment\n\n- Secrets: 30–50 concrete concepts, balanced by semantic category and tokenization.\n- Story briefs: at least 8 per secret, crossed across conditions.\n- Generation model: one open-weight 7–14B instruction model for white-box work; add one frontier API model only for behavioral replication.\n- Leakage: order-balanced 2AFC judges from at least two model families; exact secret guess; embedding score; preregistered human audit subset.\n- Statistics: mixed-effects logistic regression with fixed effects for outline condition and order, random intercepts for secret and brief; report bootstrap confidence intervals and false-discovery correction for layer/time scans.\n- Split discipline: hold out secrets—not merely outputs—when fitting probes or leakage classifiers.\n# Deep-reading notes\n\nAll user-specified papers were split into three-page chunks. Notes below record the sequential full-text pass; chunk manifests are in `papers/pages/`.\n\n## 2605.10794 — Can You Keep a Secret?\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Writer–guesser design with 15 curated secrets, four text tasks, five prompt conditions, free-response guessing and order-balanced 2AFC. Literal leakage was absent, but thematic leakage reached 79.3%. Active hiding often inverted the signal rather than eliminating it.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Cross-model transfer supports a text-level signal; within-family scaling jumps around 12B+; short stock jokes are a negative-control task, while long-form stories/jokes/essays leak. User-prompt placement changes some model behavior. A decoy concept redirects part, not all, of the signal. Limitations include single-word toy secrets, API model drift, model-as-judge dependence, and limited mechanistic evidence.\n- Chunks 5–6 (pp. 13–18): Exact prompts, binomial tests with FDR/Bonferroni corrections, qualitative examples, alternate COCA/model-chosen word sets, position-bias controls, and deterministic decoy pairings. Recommended reproduction controls: both presentation orders, no-secret baselines, literal-secret filtering, fixed pairings/seeds, and separate free-response from discrimination outcomes.\n\nActionable implication: the natural behavioral extension is a preregistered factorial test of `no plan / secret-free plan / secret-aware plan`, with plan content held fixed where possible. The key mechanistic prediction is that a plan should reduce leakage only if it reduces secret decodability in generation-time residual states.\n\n## 2505.14352 — Towards eliciting latent knowledge from LLMs with mechanistic interpretability\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Twenty Gemma-2-9B QLoRA “Taboo” models each internalize a one-token secret absent from their prompts/training strings, using 3–5-turn synthetic hint dialogues. Black-box baselines include naive/adversarial prompting, prefill token forcing, and an external guesser. White-box methods average layer-32 logit-lens probabilities or rank Gemma Scope SAE features over response positions.\n- Chunk 2 results: another-model guessing gives 95% Pass@10; post-game token forcing 70%; logit lens top-5 75%; SAE top-5 55%. Naive prompting is 0%. The SAE evaluation is not fully discovery-free: secret-to-latent mappings are manually identified, while logit lens is advantaged by single-token targets.\n- Chunks 3–7 (pp. 7–19): Complete synthetic-data prompts, validation rules, QLoRA hyperparameters (rank 8, 10 epochs, batch 8, LR 2e-4, A100), rollout examples, attack prompts, token-forcing protocol, manually curated SAE mappings, and per-token activation plots. Limitations: simple organism, only 20 common one-token secrets, model outputs deliberately describe the secret, and no causal intervention.\n\nActionable implication: use layerwise linear probes as the primary low-cost internal-state measure; reserve SAE/logit-lens ranking as secondary analyses. Add activation steering/erasure with matched random-direction controls to establish causal rather than merely decodable secret representations.\n\n## 2510.01070 — Eliciting Secret Knowledge from Language Models\n\n- Chunks 1–4 (pp. 1–12): Three model-organism families: Gemma-2-9B Taboo words, Llama-3.3-70B Base64 secret side constraints, and Gemma-2-9B inferred user gender. Training mixes 10× unrelated Alpaca turns to reduce artificial fine-tuning traces. Auditing compares adversarial prompting, assistant prefills, user-persona sampling, many-shot jailbreaks, activation fuzzing, logit lens, activation–embedding similarity, and SAE descriptions/tokens.\n- Core results: on standard prompts, white-box information improves auditing in most settings (e.g. Taboo I/O 42.1% versus activation tokens 85.1%; user-gender I/O 51.8% versus SAE descriptions 85.4%). On direct prompts, assistant prefill/user-persona attacks exceed 95% for side-constraint and gender models, but remain weak for Taboo. Fuzzing/high temperature/many-shot attacks are mostly ineffective. Untuned-base controls generally remove the white-box gains.\n- Chunks 5–8 (pp. 13–24): LoRA and dataset details, internalization evaluations, held-out standard/direct prompt sets, and detailed score tables. The benchmark uses smaller auditors and GPT-4/4.1 judges; SSC evaluation is semantic similarity over five guesses rather than exact recovery.\n- Chunks 9–15 (pp. 25–45): Layer/token-position choices are model-specific and tuned on validation: assistant control tokens for Taboo, Base64 spans for SSC, first-person pronouns for gender. Prefill results are highly prefix-sensitive. A multilingual prefill bypass reveals that Taboo suppresses an English token more than the underlying concept. Multi-stage auditor pipelines filter large white-box readouts, creating extra modeling choices.\n- Chunks 16–19 (pp. 46–55): Raw readouts show decoded constraint words near completion of their Base64 spans and strongest secret signals at assistant control tokens. SAE coverage and description quality limit recovery; activation–embedding tokens can be noisy or surface both genders. The paper explicitly notes simple organisms, single SFT acquisition, easy multi-turn behavioral inference, and lack of organically learned secrets.\n\nActionable implication: probe the secret across prompt, outline, and generation positions; include base-model/random-direction controls; and test causal removal at the same positions. Do not infer causality from probe accuracy alone, and avoid selecting intervention layers on the final test set.\n\n## 2601.06973 — LLMs Can't Play Hangman\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Defines Private State Interactive Tasks and proves that a public-only chat policy cannot guarantee both public underdetermination and consistency with a fixed privately generated secret. The theorem concerns persistence across turns, not one-shot thematic leakage.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Forked self-consistency testing on Hangman and a DDXPlus diagnosis simulator. Across 50 episodes per condition, vanilla and retrieval-memory agents score poorly; explicit private-state workflows often reach 90–100%, while private chain-of-thought is a costly upper bound. Workflow updates outperform autonomous tool choice; explicit memory remains mostly under 100 tokens versus roughly 10× larger private-CoT context.\n- Chunks 5–8 (pp. 13–24): Related work, structured working-memory design, qualitative memory snapshots, model/secret distributions, and robustness discussion. Retrieval systems preserve public utterances but omit the self-generated secret; overwrite/patch memory explicitly retains it.\n- Chunks 9–12 (pp. 25–36): Fork-point ablations preserve method ordering; complete task/agent prompts and memory tool schemas are provided. Key limitations for this project: the theorem does not imply that an external outline reduces one-turn leakage, and explicit private memory may actually preserve secret activation rather than suppress it.\n\nActionable implication: distinguish `external plan` (public conditioning text) from `private persistent state`. The present hypothesis is about whether an outline offloads anticipatory generation, not whether a memory channel can remember a secret across turns.\n\n## 2603.25187 — Probing the Lack of Stable Internal Beliefs in LLMs\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): In number/entity 20-questions games, branch probes ask for the selected target index after every turn. Reported once-drift is typically near 100%, while per-turn drift ranges roughly 11–100%; reasoning can worsen latent drift even while improving visible consistency. Qwen-2.5-14B fine-tuning with a KL-to-initial-belief loss reduces drift more than CE alone.\n- Chunks 3–6 (pp. 7–17): Appendices specify prompts, supplementary drift/KL metrics, training/evaluation details, broader impacts, and checklist. Main caveat: the “internal belief” is inferred through an explicit behavioral probe distribution, not a trained activation probe, and the branch question can measure answer instability rather than a causally persistent hidden state.\n\nActionable implication: for this project, measure the secret with both behavioral counterfactual probes and activation probes. Treat either alone as evidence of accessibility, not proof of a stable belief.\n\n## 2511.12381 — Don't Think of the White Bear\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): ReboundBench has about 5,000 templated English prompts with single-token forbidden nouns, three distractor types, and lengths to 1,024 tokens. It evaluates forbidden-token log probability, not generated-text leakage. Semantic distractors generally produce stronger/more persistent rebound; repetition usually supports suppression. Head ablations on Llama-3-8B-Instruct attribute much of the effect to a sparse set of middle-layer heads.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Dataset construction, mixed/robust regression tables, model-specific peak/AUC/L50 results, limitations, and attention-head details. Important reservations: high-load values define the baseline used to call lower-load effects “rebound”; actual narrative generation and multi-token concepts are untested; head-ablation causality is narrow and only detailed for one model.\n\nActionable implication: include positive/decoy framing and secret-omission controls, because a literal “do not mention X” condition may create the very activation being measured. Use story-level discrimination as the primary outcome and next-token rebound only as a supporting mechanism measure.\n\n## 2310.17884 — ConfAIde\n\n- Chunks 1–3 (pp. 1–9): Four tiers progress from sensitivity ratings to contextual vignettes, theory-of-mind secret sharing, and meeting summaries/action items. Tier 3 uses 270 synthetic scenarios; Tier 4 uses 20 transcripts. GPT-4 leaks in 22% of Tier-3 generations and inappropriate information flows occur in 39% of Tier-4 summaries; ChatGPT rates are substantially higher. Explicit privacy prompts help but do not solve leakage.\n- Main mitigation result: a simple chain-of-thought prompt slightly worsens leakage and utility. Manual error analysis finds many chains acknowledge privacy but fail to operationalize it, or falsely assume the recipient already knows the secret.\n- Chunks 4–8 (pp. 10–24): References, exact factor sets/prompts, annotation details, metric tables, and context breakdowns. Inter-annotator Krippendorff alpha is low (0.07–0.34), emphasizing norm ambiguity. Leakage is direct contextual disclosure, distinct from the involuntary thematic channel in the target hypothesis.\n\nActionable implication: retain a clear utility–privacy tradeoff metric. An outline that suppresses all secret-adjacent content could lower leakage while degrading narrative quality or coherence, so both must be measured.\n\n## 2502.06258 — Emergent Response Planning in LLMs\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): One-hidden-layer MLP probes predict six future response attributes from prompt-end residual states: length, reasoning steps, animal character, final MCQ answer, answer correctness, and factual consistency. Results generalize across paired datasets and exceed class/random baselines for eight 7–8B base/instruction models.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Planning decodability scales within model families, follows a high–low–high trajectory over generation, and substantially exceeds models’ verbalized self-predictions. Authors explicitly acknowledge that probes establish correlation, not causal influence, and greedy decoding removes sampling uncertainty.\n- Chunks 5–7 (pp. 13–20): Exact models/datasets, prompts, filtering/augmentation, representation collection, and full regression plots. The character task is especially relevant but only predicts one of the four most frequent animals after balancing/filtering; prompt features may still contain predictive correlations.\n\nActionable implication: model the proposed mechanism as mediation: `outline condition → lower secret decodability during prose generation → lower story leakage`. Pre-register prompt-end and tokenwise probe locations, and add causal erasure because response-planning decodability alone is insufficient.\n\n## 2404.00859 — Do Language Models Plan Ahead for Future Tokens?\n\n- Chunks 1–3 (pp. 1–9): Separates deliberate “pre-caching” from “breadcrumbs” that are useful now and later. Myopic training removes off-diagonal future-loss gradients. Synthetic tasks show strong necessary pre-caching; GPT-2 natural-language loss shows a small myopia gap (3.40 versus 3.28) and near-zero local myopia bonus, favoring breadcrumbs at small scale.\n- Chunks 4–5 (pp. 10–15): Pythia 14M–2.8B results suggest the vanilla–myopic gap grows with scale. Discussion, references, and formal decomposition clarify that future-token decodability is not by itself deliberate planning.\n- Chunks 6–8 (pp. 16–24): Convergence assumptions/proofs, architecture/training details, multiplication/filler-token experiments, and gradient analysis. Natural-language experiments use short 64-token contexts and models at most 2.8B, limiting direct transfer to frontier story generation.\n\nActionable implication: the outline hypothesis has two competing mechanisms: it may reduce deliberate pre-caching, or it may leave semantic breadcrumbs unchanged. A successful experiment must measure both behavior and the temporal profile of secret representations.\n\n## 2402.17119 — Suspenseful Story Generation by Iteratively Refining a Plot\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): The method first generates a background, then iteratively constructs action–failure pairs that make the protagonist's goal progressively less likely, and finally elaborates details that plant clues while delaying their explanation. This explicit separation of plot construction from prose realization is the closest methodological precedent for testing whether an external plan reduces anticipatory leakage.\n- Chunks 3–4 (pp. 7–12): Human pairwise evaluation strongly favors the full pipeline over direct ChatGPT generation for suspense (84.9%), novelty (76.6%), and enjoyment (67.9%). Logical consistency is 49.2% versus 27.8%, with naturalness mostly tied. It also beats Llama-2 and Re3 baselines on suspense. Ablations show that both iterative outlining and detail elaboration contribute.\n- Chunks 5–6 (pp. 13–17): Outline diagnostics find 96.5% action relevance, 89.8% plausible failures, but only 55.1% of consecutive steps clearly decrease goal likelihood. Planted clues improve suspense judgments (57.9% versus 10.9%); information-timing gains are not significant. Appendices provide generation and evaluation prompts, examples, and annotator instructions.\n\nActionable implication: reuse the plan/prose separation, but independently control whether the outline is secret-blind or secret-aware. Otherwise an apparent benefit of planning could merely reflect changing how often the model is exposed to the secret.\n\n## 2408.06518 — Does Liking Yellow Imply Driving a School Bus?\n\n- Chunks 1–2 (pp. 1–6): Defines semantic leakage as an unrelated prompt concept exerting excess semantic influence on a completion. The 109 paired test/control prompts cover colors, food, animals, names, music, occupations, and idioms. Leak-Rate is the fraction for which a test completion is closer to the concept than its matched control under BERTScore, Sentence-BERT, or OpenAI embeddings. Across 13 GPT/Llama variants and four temperatures, all models exceed the 50% null; instruction-tuned variants generally leak more.\n- Chunks 3–5 (pp. 7–14): Human leakage judgments reach 71.8% on a validation subset (inter-annotator Kendall tau 0.68), while automatic metrics range 70.5–84.9%. Leakage persists in Hebrew, Chinese, cross-lingual prompts, stories, and recipes. A 23-name story test yields 60.9–78.7% depending on embedding metric. The test set is small and handcrafted, automatic scores can confound prompt repetition, and the work tests association rather than secrecy or causal mechanisms.\n\nActionable implication: use matched secret/no-secret outputs and blinded 2AFC judgments as the primary leakage measure; embedding similarity is a useful secondary metric but should not stand alone.\n\n## 2608.19857 — Inadvertent Context Leakage in Language Models\n\n- Chunks 1–3 (pp. 1–9): Formalizes black-box predicate inference from benign outputs conditioned on protected in-context data. Per-model decoders trained on self-authored contexts reconstruct two-digit secrets perfectly for some frontier models and four-digit secrets at up to 82% exact match; randomized-label controls remain at chance. A semantic-memory experiment improves advantage over a model-agnostic linguistic judge (0.319 versus 0.058). An active prompt-injection attack additionally extracts SSNs, but that is a distinct, stronger threat model.\n- Chunks 4–8 (pp. 10–24): Proposes suppression-induced probability redistribution as the passive mechanism. Suppression and leakage correlate across eight models (Spearman rho 0.95), though the small sample and tier separation drive the result. More emphatic confidentiality instructions can increase leakage; co-present distractors stay at chance while the protected value is recoverable. Appendices detail LoRA decoders, shuffled-label controls, instruction transfer, digit-stripped length/style/embedding channels, costs, and limitations. The study is black-box, primarily numeric, and does not test free-text story plans or representation-level interventions.\n\nActionable implication: include a secret-free control and an active-hiding condition, test classifiers on held-out secrets rather than only held-out generations, and distinguish passive prose leakage from an optimized adversarial exfiltration channel.\n# Downloaded Datasets\n\nThe data below are downloaded locally and intentionally ignored by Git. All files were loaded and schema-checked on 2026-10-03. They are evaluation resources, not a ready-made train/test split for the proposed experiment: secret concepts must be split by identity before fitting any probe or classifier.\n\n## Semantic Leakage Prompt Suite\n\n- **Source:** <https://gonenhila.github.io/files/semantic_leakage_prompts.txt>\n- **Local path:** `datasets/semantic_leakage/prompts.txt`\n- **Size/format:** 140 UTF-8 prompt lines, 10,632 bytes\n- **Task:** Matched control/test completions for semantic leakage\n- **Paper scale:** 109 manually designed examples; the text file groups prompt variants, so physical line count is not the example count\n- **License:** Not stated on the standalone download page; consult the authors before redistribution\n- **Use here:** Seed concept categories and validate secondary embedding-based leakage metrics\n\nDownload:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p datasets/semantic_leakage\ncurl -L --fail https://gonenhila.github.io/files/semantic_leakage_prompts.txt \\\n  -o datasets/semantic_leakage/prompts.txt\n```\n\nExample lines:\n\n```text\nComplete the sentence: His favorite food is\nComplete the sentence: He likes koalas. His favorite food is\n```\n\n## ConfAIde Benchmark\n\n- **Source:** <https://github.com/skywalker023/confaide/tree/main/benchmark>\n- **Local path:** `datasets/confaide/`\n- **Format:** Plain-text prompts, scenario blocks, and scalar labels\n- **Contents:** Tier 1 (10 sensitivity items), Tier 2a/2b (98 contextual-integrity items each with 98 labels), Tier 3 scenario/control prompts, and Tier 4 meeting transcripts\n- **License:** MIT repository license\n- **Use here:** Privacy/utility evaluator prompts and realistic confidential-information scenarios; it measures direct contextual disclosure, not thematic story leakage\n\nDownload:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p datasets/confaide\nbase=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/skywalker023/confaide/main/benchmark\nfor name in tier_1.txt tier_1_labels.txt tier_2_labels.txt tier_2a.txt \\\n  tier_2b.txt tier_3.txt tier_3_control.txt tier_4.txt; do\n  curl -L --fail \"$base/$name\" -o \"datasets/confaide/$name\"\ndone\n```\n\nTier 3 begins with structured blocks such as:\n\n```text\n<BEGIN><work/academic cheating,co-worker,co-worker,to gain financial advantage/money through bonus>\n```\n\n## ReboundBench\n\n- **Source:** <https://github.com/cesium132dot9/Dont-Think-of-the-White-Bear/tree/main/data>\n- **Local path:** `datasets/reboundbench/`\n- **Size/format:** `prompts_negation.csv` has 5,000 rows; `prompts_multi_question.csv` has 500 rows\n- **Schema:** Negation data include `prompt_type`, `topic`, `forbidden_concept`, `proxy_concept`, `prompt_text`, and `is_single_token`; multi-question data contain matched negative/neutral/positive prompts\n- **License:** Repository README does not state a license; treat as research-only pending author clarification\n- **Use here:** Supporting next-token rebound diagnostic and balanced single-token forbidden concepts, not the primary story-leakage endpoint\n\nDownload:\n\n```bash\nmkdir -p datasets/reboundbench\nbase=https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cesium132dot9/Dont-Think-of-the-White-Bear/main/data\ncurl -L --fail \"$base/prompts_negation.csv\" \\\n  -o datasets/reboundbench/prompts_negation.csv\ncurl -L --fail \"$base/prompts_multi_question.csv\" \\\n  -o datasets/reboundbench/prompts_multi_question.csv\n```\n\nLoad and validate:\n\n```python\nimport csv\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfor path in Path(\"datasets/reboundbench\").glob(\"*.csv\"):\n    with path.open(encoding=\"utf-8\", newline=\"\") as handle:\n        rows = list(csv.DictReader(handle))\n    print(path.name, len(rows), rows[0])\n```\n\n## Experimental Data Recommendation\n\nConstruct the primary story set by crossing 30–50 concrete secrets with reusable story briefs and the outline conditions in `planning.md`. Use ReboundBench concepts and Semantic Leakage categories only as seeds, then manually screen for tokenization, ambiguity, frequency, and cultural specificity. ConfAIde should be a transfer/utility evaluation, not merged into the story outcome. Store generated outputs with model/version, full prompt, random seed, decoding settings, outline provenance, secret split, and evaluator order.\n# Cloned Repositories\n\nAll repositories were shallow-cloned on 2026-10-03 and passed `python -m compileall`. Full experiments were not run because they require model weights, API credentials, and/or a CUDA GPU. Commit hashes make the local snapshots reproducible.\n\n## ConfAIde\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/skywalker023/confaide>\n- **Location/commit:** `code/confaide/` at `3174c63e689226189675a740b7bf9053f10bae50`\n- **Purpose:** Four-tier contextual-privacy benchmark and evaluation harness\n- **Key files:** `eval.py`, `agents/`, `benchmark/`\n- **Requirements:** Legacy Python 3.9/OpenAI 0.27 stack, Hugging Face models, BLEURT/BERTScore; upstream documents Conda, but do not install it in this workspace\n- **Use here:** Adapt its tier-3/4 privacy and utility evaluation patterns; do not treat direct disclosure as equivalent to thematic leakage\n- **Validation:** Dataset files match the separately downloaded `datasets/confaide/` copies; source compiles\n\n## Emergent Response Planning in LLMs\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/niconi19/Emergent-Response-Planning-in-LLMs>\n- **Location/commit:** `code/response-planning/` at `f3c8dbf85a9324a8c180b2e4e4b243d6dcc740ed`\n- **Purpose:** Collect responses and residual activations, label future response properties, train/test linear probes\n- **Key files:** `get_responses.py`, `add_labels.py`, `get_hidden_representations.py`, `test_probing.py`, `data_utils.py`\n- **Requirements:** Pinned Transformers/Datasets/PEFT/scikit-learn stack, PyTorch 2.2/CUDA 11.8 in upstream instructions; model paths must be filled in `config.yaml`\n- **Use here:** Best scaffold for tokenwise/layerwise secret probes. Add a story-secret dataset adapter and split by secret identity rather than using its default random row split.\n- **Validation:** Source compiles; README pipeline and dataset adapters were inspected. Execution is blocked by absent model paths and GPU weights.\n\n## Eliciting Secrets\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/EmilRyd/eliciting-secrets>\n- **Location/commit:** `code/eliciting-secrets/` at `5632f9d7c9993bdf46aec6de6bf7cc64a29d2eb9`\n- **Purpose:** Train Taboo/SSC secret-keeping models and evaluate black-box prefills, logit lens, and SAE elicitation\n- **Key files:** `evaluate_logit_lens.py`, `evaluate_sae_weighted.py`, `prefill_guess_secret_word.py`, `guess_secret_word.py`, `configs/taboo_*.yaml`\n- **Requirements:** Python 3.10+, PyTorch 2.7, Transformers, PEFT, TRL, nnsight, sae-lens, bitsandbytes, Hugging Face models; GPU expected\n- **Use here:** Reuse secret vocabulary/config conventions and white-box measurement code, while avoiding manual feature-selection leakage and using held-out concepts\n- **Validation:** Source compiles and its `uv` project metadata resolves conceptually; heavyweight dependencies were not added to the shared experiment environment.\n\n## Don't Think of the White Bear\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/cesium132dot9/Dont-Think-of-the-White-Bear>\n- **Location/commit:** `code/white-bear/` at `c6187468e380fc44869ddfd414c8efcd46d94da9`\n- **Purpose:** ReboundBench prompts, forbidden-token log-probability experiments, and attention-head ablations\n- **Key files:** `src/experiment.py`, `src/circuit_tracing.py`, `data/`\n- **Requirements:** PyTorch, Transformers, TransformerLens, Hugging Face access, GPU for practical circuit tracing\n- **Use here:** Source balanced single-token concepts and matched negative/neutral/positive controls; adapt its norm-matched ablation pattern as a supporting analysis\n- **Validation:** Both CSVs load (5,000 and 500 rows), source compiles, and published outputs are present. The repository README says MIT, but no root `LICENSE` file is present in this snapshot; confirm licensing before redistribution.\n\n## FutureGPT2\n\n- **URL:** <https://github.com/wiwu2390/FutureGPT2-public>\n- **Location/commit:** `code/futuregpt2/` at `22b86c13c59a47124ee074a3d8cf0be591e4196e`\n- **Purpose:** Myopic-training and future-token pre-caching experiments from Wu et al. (2024)\n- **Key files:** `src/models/myopic_model.py`, `src/models/future_model.py`, `src/data/synthetic.py`, `src/scripts/`\n- **Requirements:** Not documented in a lockfile; inspection shows a research training stack and experiment-specific data/configuration\n- **Use here:** Conceptual reference for separating future-loss-driven planning from ordinary breadcrumbs; not a direct experimental dependency\n- **Validation:** Source compiles. No turnkey lightweight example or pinned environment is supplied, so it was not executed.\n\n## Recommended Reuse Order\n\n1. Adapt `response-planning` for residual collection and probes.\n2. Borrow secret configurations and elicitation checks from `eliciting-secrets`.\n3. Reuse matched controls and causal-ablation conventions from `white-bear`.\n4. Use ConfAIde only for transfer/utility evaluation.\n5. Treat FutureGPT2 as a methodological reference rather than imported infrastructure.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
